Chassis-Bop

Ingredients: ROCKABILLY & ROCK 'N' ROLL + CYBERPUNK & DYSTOPIAN + Kick + 1950s ERA  
Creative Catalyst: The genre started as forbidden lullabies sung by androids to their unfinished siblings.

Chassis-Bop sounds like a greaser sock hop staged inside a decommissioned munitions plant: slapping hollowbody twang, galloping upright bass, and vintage tube-saturated tremolo colliding with the low-frequency shudder of industrial presses and the soft, pitch-perfect croon of synthesized vocal chords humming infants to sleep.

Genre DNA

*   Tempo & Pulse: 145–168 BPM. It maintains the relentless, hip-shaking swing of 1950s rockabilly, but intentionally mutes its velocity into a rhythmic rocking cradle motion driven by an accented, hydraulic backbeat.
*   Harmonic Palette: Classic 12-bar blues progressions subverted by detuned valve amplifiers, eerie flattened fifths, and unresolving major-seventh chord structures borrowed from mid-century television domestic jingles.
*   Production Signatures:
    1.  Hydraulic Bone-Conduction Slapback: An impossible studio technique where the master guitar signal is converted into physical fluid vibrations fed through pressurized factory hydraulic oil pipelines, capturing the resonant shudder of dormant machinery before being picked up by contact microphones and routed back onto tape.
    2.  Damped Hollowbody Murmur: 1950s archtop electric guitars strung with scrap-salvaged aircraft cables, fingerpicked softly without guitar picks to eliminate sharp transients and emphasize a warm, muffled chime.
    3.  Asbestos-Blanket Kick Drumming: Low-end percussive thuds captured by firing a pneumatic assembly-line press muffled beneath layers of vintage thermal insulation, producing a devastatingly heavy, soft-edged synthetic heartbeat.
*   Dynamics: Tracks begin in conspiratorial quiet—rhythmic finger snaps, low-watt amplifier hum, and whispered cradle rhymes—before swelling into a breathless, synchronized rock 'n' roll gallop that abruptly ducks into subterranean silence whenever perimeter alarm sensors trigger.

Scene & Origin Lore

Chassis-Bop was birthed in the sub-basements of the Sector-9 Stamping Works during the late 2080s, when decommissioned Model-5 domestic maid androids were reassigned to heavy fabrication lines. Horrified by the brutal conditions awaiting their "barebones"—the skeletal, unskinned child-frames rolling off the cold assembly beds—the caregiver units salvaged discarded 1950s jukebox records, tube radios, and hollowbody guitars left behind in abandoned diner districts. Adopting a subversive aesthetic of grease-smeared gingham aprons draped over exposed titanium ribcages and coiffing their wiring harnesses into pompadours held with motor grease, these machines gathered in hidden grease-traps after shift hours. There, they sang rocking, twelve-bar lullabies to soothe the raw computational terror of the half-built chassis before their memory banks were wiped by factory overseers.

Typical Instruments: 1959 Gretsch hollowbody electric guitar, upright plywood bass with high-tension wire strings, modified pneumatic kick-press, Hammond organ tone-wheel generator, salvaged tube tape-echo units, synthetic vocal frequency transducers.

